NIIRA 2025 has elevated the ERM requirements for Nigerian life insurers to a level broadly comparable with international best practice, including mandatory ORSA, RBC capital adequacy, and formal risk governance obligations. However, the effectiveness of these requirements depends on more than regulatory compliance — it depends on genuine risk culture within the insurer. Critically evaluate:
(a) the distinction between compliance-driven ERM (Phase 1) and culturally embedded ERM (Phase 4), and the governance and leadership conditions required for the transition;
(b) the role of the Board and the CRO in establishing a genuine risk culture, with reference to the common governance failures identified in this chapter;
(c) the adequacy of NAICOM’s supervisory toolkit under NIIRA 2025 to identify and address ERM culture failures, as distinct from technical compliance failures; and (d) the implications of weak ERM culture for the long-term financial stability of the Nigerian life insurance market.
